### (1/2) Keep temperature polling resilient per adapter

**Severity:** `medium`

A driver/API exception for one LUID currently aborts the whole cycle before any values are committed, so the failing GPU and all later adapters retain stale readings. Catch failures inside the loop, mark that adapter unavailable, and continue so the remaining sampled GPUs refresh. Apply this together with (2/2), which adds the regression test.

```suggestion
        foreach (var luid in luids)
        {
            try
            {
                temperatures[luid] = _readTemperature(luid);
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                LogFailureOnce(ref _temperatureReadFailureLogged, "Failed while reading GPU temperatures.", ex);
                temperatures[luid] = 0;
            }
        }
```

### (2/2) Cover an isolated adapter read failure

**Severity:** `medium`

The existing tests cover successful readings and unavailable adapters, but not a thrown read after values were already displayed. This two-adapter test verifies that the failing GPU becomes unavailable and a later adapter still refreshes; apply it together with (1/2).

```suggestion

    [TestMethod]
    public void GetGPUTemperature_ReadFailureDoesNotLeaveStaleTemperatureOrSkipLaterAdapters()
    {
        var failFirstRead = false;
        using var stats = new GPUStats(
            new()
            {
                [1] = new("NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090", IsSoftware: false),
                [2] = new("AMD Radeon(TM) Graphics", IsSoftware: false),
            },
            [1, 2],
            luid =>
            {
                if (luid == 1 && failFirstRead)
                {
                    throw new System.InvalidOperationException();
                }

                return luid == 1 ? 44.4f : (failFirstRead ? 57f : 56f);
            });

        stats.RequestDetails(true);
        stats.UpdateTemperatures();

        Assert.AreEqual("44 °C", stats.GetGPUTemperature(0));
        Assert.AreEqual("56 °C", stats.GetGPUTemperature(1));

        failFirstRead = true;
        stats.UpdateTemperatures();

        Assert.AreEqual("--", stats.GetGPUTemperature(0));
        Assert.AreEqual("57 °C", stats.GetGPUTemperature(1));
    }
}
```

### Detect mismatched detail requests during development

**Severity:** `low`

The production clamp protects the counter from remaining negative, but silently hides an unmatched `RequestDetails(false)` and makes lifecycle-pairing regressions harder to diagnose. Capture the decrement and assert in Debug while retaining the existing production clamp.

```suggestion
        else
        {
            var requestCount = Interlocked.Decrement(ref _detailRequests);
            Debug.Assert(requestCount >= 0, "GPU detail request count must not go below zero.");
            if (requestCount < 0)
            {
                Interlocked.Exchange(ref _detailRequests, 0);
            }
        }
```
